{"causes": ["The Spanish War of Independence (1808-1814) exposed the fragility of the Bourbon monarchy and fostered early liberal movements, shaping Calatrava's political consciousness.", "The Liberal Triennium (1820-1823) provided Calatrava with his first significant experience in parliamentary politics and government, serving as Minister of Justice.", "The death of Fernando VII in 1833 and the subsequent succession crisis between Isabella II and Don Carlos created a political vacuum that empowered liberal factions, including Calatrava, to lead the regency government."], "effects": ["Calatrava's government enacted the Constitution of 1837, which served as a foundational legal document for Spain's liberal state for decades.", "His administration furthered the disentailment of church lands (desamortización de Mendizábal), fundamentally restructuring Spanish land ownership and economic power.", "Calatrava's tenure as Prime Minister helped stabilize the regency of María Cristina and consolidate the progressive liberal faction's influence in the nascent constitutional monarchy."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "fernando-vii-of-spain", "sourceName": "Fernando VII of Spain", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "jos-mara-calatrava-y-peinado", "targetName": "José María Calatrava y Peinado", "context": "The absolutist reign of Fernando VII led to Calatrava's exile and strengthened his commitment to liberal constitutionalism between 1823 and 1833."}, {"sourceSlug": "jos-mara-calatrava-y-peinado", "sourceName": "José María Calatrava y Peinado", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "first-carlist-war", "targetName": "First Carlist War", "context": "Calatrava served as Prime Minister of Spain (1836-1837) during a critical phase of the First Carlist War, focusing his government's efforts on financing the conflict and solidifying liberal rule."}, {"sourceSlug": "jos-mara-calatrava-y-peinado", "sourceName": "José María Calatrava y Peinado", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "